Round gen_r9 complete. The node never became available (both Ice Lake nodes held by other users all round, per the monitor's NOTICE), so this was a model-side round: I built the r8-queued 2-wide RP3_WINO kernel for p=103, proved it bit-identical to the r8 engine across all 30 class primes, then **defaulted it OFF on measurement** — wallaby turns out to be a Sapphire Rapids (a graded xarch machine), and the paired kernel lost +35% there (3/3, per-pass: x +29%, y +35%, z +74%) while a calibration control (the node-proven rp2 pairing win at 113) showed the host's anti-pairing bias is only ~3 points — so the loss is real, caused by rp3's ~2x worse glue-to-conv ratio. The shipped binary is bit-for-bit the r8 engine everywhere; the ICL race (2-wide, prefetch-at-103, 2-wide-at-97 arms) is fully staged in `r9_ab.sh` for the moment the hold lands. Along the way I caught and documented a harness trap: `impl` is a symlink to the live `impl_9`, so my first "control" build was the edited source — all verdicts were re-established against the true r8 binary. The avenue-1 duty (bank the picks) is discharged: create() is deterministic by construction, verified 5/5 identical cycles.

**Ended on: 2-wide RP3_WINO at p=103, shipped default-OFF behind -DRP_W23 after a calibrated SPR loss (+35% vs the +2.8% bias ceiling), engine otherwise bit-identical to r8 — graded cell L=31: 84.745 us/step B=16 / 85.21 B=1 on the node board (65.0 / 64.6 us on wallaby-SPR this session), rel L2 4.059e-16 single / 1.784e-15 two-step.**
